        "original_id": "fd30a4e9-3531-5c86-902e-3670aa0b3e8b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: King Conchobar, Cúchulainn, Queen Medb\n- Places: Ulster, Connacht, Cuailgne, Ireland, Lowland Scotland\n- Works: Louis le Brocquy Táin illustrations\n- Languages: Scots, Ulster Scots, Irish\n- Topics: Táin narrative, Ulster province\n\nFacts:\n- The Louis le Brocquy Táin illustrations page says the Táin tells of the exploits of King Conchobar and his chief warrior Cúchulainn, known as \"The Hound of Ulster.\"\n- The Louis le Brocquy Táin illustrations page says the Táin also tells of Queen Medb of Connacht invading Ulster to capture the Brown Bull of Cuailgne.\n- Ulster is a province in the north of the island of Ireland.\n- The Irish names for Ulster are \"Ulaidh\" and \"Cúige Uladh.\"\n- Scots is a Germanic language variety spoken in Lowland Scotland and in parts of Ulster.\n- In parts of Ulster, the local Scots dialect is known as Ulster Scots.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
